 Exxon and Chevron you  could say that both BP and uh shell are  far more investing far more heavily in  Renewables than the likes of their  American counterparts all of whom are  much more profitable I should say  Chevron has just reported a quarterly  profit of 11.2 billion US Dollars uh  Exxon has just reported a quarterly  profit of 20 billion dollars so  significantly higher than the 8.15  billion dollars that BP has just  reported I mean you mentioned some of  the highlights there I mean in the  latest quarter they've completed an  investment in a big green hydrogen  project in Australia and the pilbara  region of Western Western Australia  which is expected to be one of the  biggest such projects in the world  they've also invested in a green  hydrogen uh project in t side in this  country which again is one of the  largest of its kind and BP pulse the  charging Network to which you referred  well the number of charging points for  electric vehicles that BP has got  operating now is up some 60 on this time  last year so you could argue they're  making pretty rapid progress in that  sense the key headline numbers you've  got to look at is that by 2025 BP say  that 40 of all their capital expenditure  is going to be on low carbon and  renewable projects and by the end of the  decade it will represent 50 percent of  all capital expenditures so they are  pivoting a very pretty rapidly away from  traditional fossil fuel activities and  you'll recall of course more than a year  ago Bernard Looney the chief executive  made clear that BP would actually be  leaving some of the oil and gas reserves  that it owns in the ground because it  wants to concentrate on those low carbon  operations on the point of a windfall  tax well it's actually very very  debatable that the government can go  much further than it has the Chief  Financial Officer Murray Hocking class  today making the point that for every  three dollars BP currently makes in the  North Sea two dollars of tha